= Solution
{parent=1}

The <binomial theorem> gives $(1+x)^8=\sum_{k=0}^8\binom8k x^k$. The required <binomial coefficients> are therefore
$$
\boxed{[x^4](1+x)^8=\binom84=70,\qquad [x^5](1+x)^8=\binom85=56.}
$$
Here $[x^k]$ denotes the coefficient of $x^k$ in the <polynomial>.

= 2
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The given <polynomial root> supplies the factor $2x-3$. <Polynomial division> gives
$$
2x^3-5x^2+x+3=(2x-3)(x^2-x-1).
$$
Solving the remaining <quadratic equation>, the other two <polynomial roots> are
$$
\boxed{x=\frac{1+\sqrt5}{2}\quad\text{and}\quad x=\frac{1-\sqrt5}{2}.}
$$

= 3
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

Use the first of the <simultaneous equations> to put $y=1-x$. Substitution into the second gives
$$
x-x^2+2(1-x)^2=2,\qquad x^2-3x=x(x-3)=0.
$$
Thus $x=0$ or $x=3$. The corresponding values of $y$ give exactly
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=(0,1)\text{ or }(3,-2).}
$$

= 4
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

<Completing the square> transforms the equation into
$$
(x+1)^2+(y+2)^2=9.
$$
Comparison with the standard <circle> equation gives
$$
\boxed{\text{centre}=(-1,-2),\qquad\text{radius}=3.}
$$

= 5
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

The addition formula for the <sine> gives
$$
A\sin(t+\phi)=A\cos\phi\sin t+A\sin\phi\cos t.
$$
Equating the <sine> and <cosine> coefficients gives $A\cos\phi=\sqrt3$ and $A\sin\phi=1$. Squaring and adding yields $A^2=4$, so $A=2$ because $A>0$. Both coefficients are positive, so $\phi$ is in the first quadrant. Consequently
$$
\boxed{A=2,\qquad\phi=\pi/6.}
$$

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

This is a <finite geometric series> with common ratio $r=1/\sqrt2$. Since $r^{10}=1/32$,
$$
\sum_{n=1}^{10}2^{-n/2}=\frac{r(1-r^{10})}{1-r}=\frac{31}{32}\frac1{\sqrt2-1}=\frac{31+31\sqrt2}{32}.
$$
Thus $\boxed{p=31,\ q=31}$.

= 7
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

The <chain rule> and the <quotient rule> give
$$
f'(x)=\exp\left(\frac{x}{1+x^2}\right)\frac{1-x^2}{(1+x^2)^2}.
$$
The <exponential function> and denominator are positive, so the <stationary points> occur exactly at $x=\pm1$. Their values are
$$
\boxed{f(-1)=e^{-1/2},\qquad f(1)=e^{1/2}.}
$$
The <derivative> is negative for $|x|>1$ and positive for $|x|<1$, so $x=-1$ is a <local minimum> and $x=1$ a <local maximum>. They are also global extrema, since $-1/2\leq x/(1+x^2)\leq1/2$.

= 8
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

Apply <implicit differentiation>, remembering that $y$ depends on $x$:
$$
1+y'+e^x+e^y y'=0.
$$
Since $1+e^y>0$, division is always legitimate. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\frac{dy}{dx}=-\frac{1+e^x}{1+e^y}.}
$$

= 9
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

The <partial fraction decomposition> is
$$
\frac{2x+1}{x(x+1)}=\frac1x+\frac1{x+1}.
$$
Taking <antiderivatives> and using the positive endpoints gives
$$
\int_2^3\frac{2x+1}{x(x+1)}\,dx=[\ln x+\ln(x+1)]_2^3=\ln\frac{3\cdot4}{2\cdot3}=\ln2.
$$
Hence $\boxed{n=2}$.

= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=10}

The <function graphs> intersect when $2x=4xe^{-x}$, so $x=0$ or $x=\ln2$. For $0<x<\ln2$, the <exponential function> satisfies $e^{-x}>1/2$, making $4xe^{-x}$ the upper graph. The enclosed <area> is therefore
$$
\begin{aligned}
A&=\int_0^{\ln2}(4xe^{-x}-2x)\,dx\\
&=[-4(x+1)e^{-x}-x^2]_0^{\ln2}\\
&=\boxed{2-2\ln2-(\ln2)^2.}
\end{aligned}
$$
The <antiderivative> of $xe^{-x}$ follows by <integration by parts>.

