Direct multiplication, using $i^2=-1$, gives
$$
(3-2i)(4+3i)=12+9i-8i-6i^2=18+i.
$$
Thus the <real part> is $\boxed{18}$ and the <imaginary part> is $\boxed{1}$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Multiply numerator and denominator by the <complex conjugate> $4-3i$:
$$
\frac{3-2i}{4+3i}=\frac{(3-2i)(4-3i)}{4^2+3^2}=\frac{6-17i}{25}.
$$
Thus the <real part> is $\boxed{6/25}$ and the <imaginary part> is $\boxed{-17/25}$.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Since $z^2=(x^2-y^2)+2ixy$, equating its <real part> and <imaginary part> gives $x^2-y^2=2xy$, or
$$
y^2+2xy-x^2=0.
$$
The <quadratic equation> in $y$ factors as $[y-(-1+\sqrt2)x][y-(-1-\sqrt2)x]=0$. Thus the set is the union of two <straight lines> through the origin:
$$
\boxed{y=(-1+\sqrt2)x\quad\text{or}\quad y=(-1-\sqrt2)x.}
$$
Their slopes multiply to $-1$, so they are perpendicular. Their <angles> to the real axis are $\pi/8$ and $-3\pi/8$, modulo $\pi$. The origin is included.

\Image[paper-1-q11-loci.png]
{title=The two pairs of lines in the Argand diagrams of Question 11}

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The denominator requires $z\ne0$. Write $z^2=u+iv$, where $u=x^2-y^2$ and $v=2xy$. Multiplication by the nonzero denominator gives
$$
v=-i(u+iv)=v-iu,
$$
so $u=0$. Conversely, if $u=0$ and $z\ne0$, then $v\ne0$ and $v/(iv)=-i$. Thus the required <Argand diagram> consists exactly of
$$
\boxed{y=x\quad\text{or}\quad y=-x,\qquad (x,y)\ne(0,0).}
$$
These are two perpendicular <straight lines>, with the origin removed as shown in the figure in part (i).

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For real $x$, $\cosh x>0$, so multiplication by the <hyperbolic cosine> is reversible. The identities $\cosh x-\sinh x=e^{-x}$ and $\operatorname{sech}x=1/\cosh x$ give
$$
e^{-x}\cosh x=2,\qquad \frac{1+e^{-2x}}2=2.
$$
Thus $e^{-2x}=3$. The <exponential function> is injective, so the unique real solution is
$$
\boxed{x=-\frac12\ln3.}
$$

= d
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Set $w=e^z\ne0$. The <hyperbolic cosine> identity gives $w+w^{-1}=2i$, hence the <quadratic equation>
$$
w^2-2iw+1=0,\qquad w=i(1\pm\sqrt2).
$$
Let $L=\ln(1+\sqrt2)>0$. The first root has modulus $1+\sqrt2$ and <complex argument> $\pi/2$; the second has modulus $\sqrt2-1=e^{-L}$ and <complex argument> $-\pi/2$. Taking all <complex logarithm> values gives
$$
\boxed{z=L+i\left(\frac\pi2+2k\pi\right)\quad\text{or}\quad z=-L+i\left(-\frac\pi2+2k\pi\right),\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
The two families are distinct because their <real parts> are respectively $L$ and $-L$. This exhausts the solutions because both roots for $w$ and all possible <complex arguments> have been included.

= 12
{parent=Paper 1}
