= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

At height $z$, the <circular sector> has <radius> $r(z)=Re^{-z/H}$ and <angle> $\Theta(z)=2\pi-\pi z/H$. Its <area> is $\Theta(z)r(z)^2/2$, so slicing by horizontal cross-sections gives the <volume integral>
$$
V=\pi R^2\int_0^H\left(1-\frac z{2H}\right)e^{-2z/H}\,dz.
$$
Use the <substitution rule> $t=z/H$. Then
$$
V=\pi R^2H\int_0^1(1-t/2)e^{-2t}\,dt.
$$
An <integration by parts> gives the <antiderivative> $(t/4-3/8)e^{-2t}$, whose endpoint difference is $(3-e^{-2})/8$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{V=\frac{\pi R^2H}{8}(3-e^{-2}).}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At each height, the removed <circular sector> has the same <angle> and $4/5$ of the original <radius>. Its <area> and hence its <volume> are therefore multiplied by $(4/5)^2=16/25$. Equating the removed <volume> to that of the reformed solid <sphere> gives
$$
\frac43\pi a^3=\frac{16}{25}V=\frac{2\pi R^2H}{25}(3-e^{-2}).
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{a=\left[\frac{3R^2H}{50}(3-e^{-2})\right]^{1/3}.}
$$

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

At height $z$, the horizontal <triangle> has half-width $L\sqrt{1-z/(2H)}$ along $x$ and depth $D(1-z/H)$ along $y$. These shrink with $z$, so its <orthogonal projection> onto the $(x,y)$ <plane> is the base <triangle> with vertices $(-L,0)$, $(L,0)$ and $(0,D)$:
$$
0\leq y\leq D,\qquad |x|\leq L(1-y/D).
$$
The <orthogonal projection> onto the $(y,z)$ <plane> is the <triangle> with vertices $(0,0)$, $(D,0)$ and $(0,H)$:
$$
0\leq z\leq H,\qquad0\leq y\leq D(1-z/H).
$$
The <orthogonal projection> onto the $(x,z)$ <plane> is bounded by $z=0$, $z=H$ and the two curves
$$
\boxed{x=\pm L\sqrt{1-z/(2H)},\qquad0\leq z\leq H.}
$$
Equivalently, these curves lie on the <parabola> $z=2H(1-x^2/L^2)$, truncated at height $H$. In particular the top width is $\sqrt2L$, so this projection has a horizontal upper edge from $-L/\sqrt2$ to $L/\sqrt2$; it is not triangular. The top horizontal cross-section has zero depth, consistent with this edge.

\Image[paper-1-q12-projections.png]
{title=The three coordinate projections of the triangular-section solid in Question 12}

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The horizontal cross-section is a <triangle>, so its <area> is half its width times its depth:
$$
A(z)=LD\left(1-\frac zH\right)\sqrt{1-\frac z{2H}}.
$$
Thus the <volume integral>, with $t=z/H$, is
$$
V=LDH\int_0^1(1-t)\sqrt{1-t/2}\,dt.
$$
For the <substitution rule>, put $u=1-t/2$. The endpoint values are $1$ and $1/2$, and $1-t=2u-1$, giving
$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac V{LDH}&=2\int_{1/2}^1(2u-1)u^{1/2}\,du\\
&=2\left[\frac45u^{5/2}-\frac23u^{3/2}\right]_{1/2}^1\\
&=\frac{4+2\sqrt2}{15}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Therefore $\boxed{V=\dfrac{(4+2\sqrt2)LDH}{15}}$.

= 13
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

On an <interval> with $x\ne0$, division by $x^2$ gives the <separable differential equation> $y'=(4/x^2-1/x)y^2$. For $y\ne0$, take <antiderivatives>:
$$
\int\frac{dy}{y^2}=\int\left(\frac4{x^2}-\frac1x\right)dx,\qquad -\frac1y=-\frac4x-\ln|x|+C.
$$
Renaming the constant yields the explicit nonzero family
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac1{4/x+\ln|x|+C}.}
$$
For $x\ne0$, the <interval> of definition must avoid zeros of the denominator. Division by $y^2$ excluded the additional solution $\boxed{y(x)\equiv0}$, which must also be included.

The original undivided <differential equation> also makes sense at $x=0$, where it requires $y(0)=0$. For <continuation through a zero of the leading ODE coefficient>, rewrite the nonzero family as
$$
y(x)=\frac{x}{4+x\ln|x|+Cx}.
$$
It extends differentiably with $y(0)=0$ and $y'(0)=1/4$, since $y(x)/x\to1/4$. One may choose the integration constants independently on the two sides of zero and obtain a differentiable solution through zero. The zero solution also extends through zero, with <derivative> $0$; joining a nonzero branch on just one side to a zero branch on the other would fail <differentiability>.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For this <first-order linear differential equation>, an <integrating factor> on an <interval> avoiding zeros of $\cos x$ is $\cos x$. The <product rule> then gives
$$
(y\cos x)'=\cos x,\qquad y\cos x=\sin x+C.
$$
The <initial condition> gives $3/\sqrt2=1/\sqrt2+C$, hence $C=\sqrt2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac{\sin x+\sqrt2}{\cos x},\qquad-\frac\pi2<x<\frac\pi2.}
$$
The displayed <interval> is the maximal one containing the specified initial <point>, since the coefficient $\tan x$ is undefined at its endpoints.

= c
{parent=13}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the substitution $v=y/x$ for a <homogeneous first-order differential equation>. Since $y'=v+xv'$, it gives
$$
xv'=\tan v.
$$
Where $\sin v\ne0$, this <separable differential equation> integrates to $\ln|\sin v|=\ln|x|+C$, hence $\sin v=C_1x$. One can obtain the same relation without division by $\sin v$: the equation gives $(\sin v)'=(\sin v)/x$, so $\sin v=C_1x$, including $\sin v\equiv0$.

All real branches of the <inverse sine> are captured by
$$
\boxed{y(x)=x\left[k\pi+(-1)^k\arcsin(Cx)\right],\qquad C\in\mathbb R,\quad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
Each branch is used on an <interval> with $x\ne0$ and $|Cx|<1$, with fixed $k$. The strict inequality excludes zeros of $\cos(y/x)$, where the original <tangent> is undefined. When $C=0$, the formula includes all straight-line solutions $y=k\pi x$. Keeping both families of <inverse sine> branches is essential for the two different <initial conditions> below.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <initial condition> gives $C=\sin(\pi/3)=\sqrt3/2$. Since $\pi/3$ belongs to the principal <inverse sine> range, choose $k=0$ in part (i). The particular solution is
$$
\boxed{y(x)=x\arcsin\left(\frac{\sqrt3x}{2}\right),\qquad0<x<\frac2{\sqrt3}.}
$$
The upper endpoint is excluded because $y/x\to\pi/2$ there and the original <tangent> ceases to be defined.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Again $C=\sin(2\pi/3)=\sqrt3/2$, but the principal <inverse sine> would give $\pi/3$ at $x=1$. The required branch is $v=\pi-\arcsin(Cx)$, corresponding to $k=1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{y(x)=x\left[\pi-\arcsin\left(\frac{\sqrt3x}{2}\right)\right],\qquad0<x<\frac2{\sqrt3}.}
$$
At $x=1$ the bracket is $2\pi/3$, as required. Both the <initial condition> and the original <differential equation> are thus satisfied on the stated branch.

= 14
{parent=Paper 1}
