= a
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For an <exact differential form>, equality of the mixed <partial derivatives> requires $P_y=Q_x$. Here
$$
P_y=\frac{y}{a\sqrt x\sqrt{1+y^2}},\qquad Q_x=\frac{y}{2\sqrt x\sqrt{1+y^2}}.
$$
Equality for all $x>0$ and real $y$ forces $1/a=1/2$, so $\boxed{a=2}$. To construct the potential, integrate $P$ with respect to $x$:
$$
v(x,y)=\sqrt x\left(1+\sqrt{1+y^2}\right)+C(y).
$$
Differentiation with respect to $y$ gives $v_y=Q+C'(y)$, so $C'(y)=0$. Thus the most general potential on the connected domain $x>0$ is
$$
\boxed{v(x,y)=\sqrt x\left(1+\sqrt{1+y^2}\right)+C,\qquad C\in\mathbb R.}
$$
Its explicit <partial derivatives> verify sufficiency as well as necessity.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The stated <total differential> is that of $\pi r^2h$, so $V=\pi r^2h+C$. The zero-volume condition gives $C=0$. For positive $r,h$, divide the <total differential> by this <volume> to obtain
$$
\boxed{\frac{dV}{V}=2\frac{dr}{r}+\frac{dh}{h}.}
$$
For the specified small changes, $dV/V\approx2(0.01)+0.02=0.04$, so **the volume increases by approximately $4\%$**. The fractional expression requires $V>0$; at a degenerate zero-volume state it is undefined. For finite changes, the exact multiplier is $(1.01)^2(1.02)$, whose difference from $1$ is $4.0502\%$; the <total differential> gives its first-order approximation.

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $t=y/x$. The <product rule> and the <chain rule> give the <partial derivatives>
$$
u_x=\phi(t)+x\phi'(t)\left(-\frac y{x^2}\right)=\phi(t)-t\phi'(t),\qquad u_y=x\phi'(t)\frac1x=\phi'(t).
$$
Hence the terms containing $\phi'$ cancel:
$$
xu_x+yu_y=x\phi(t)-xt\phi'(t)+y\phi'(t)=x\phi(t)=\boxed{u}.
$$
This also follows from the <Euler theorem for homogeneous functions>, because $u(\lambda x,\lambda y)=\lambda u(x,y)$ for $\lambda>0$.

= d
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <chain rule> expresses the coordinate <differential operators> as
$$
\partial_x=a\partial_\xi+b\partial_\eta,\qquad \partial_y=-b\partial_\xi+a\partial_\eta.
$$
The coefficients are constant, so a second application gives
$$
u_{xx}=a^2u_{\xi\xi}+ab(u_{\xi\eta}+u_{\eta\xi})+b^2u_{\eta\eta},
$$
and
$$
u_{yy}=b^2u_{\xi\xi}-ab(u_{\xi\eta}+u_{\eta\xi})+a^2u_{\eta\eta}.
$$
Adding cancels the mixed <partial derivatives>, and $a^2+b^2=1$ gives
$$
\boxed{u_{xx}+u_{yy}=u_{\xi\xi}+u_{\eta\eta}.}
$$
The transformation has <rotation matrix> $\begin{pmatrix}a&-b\\b&a\end{pmatrix}$, so this identity expresses the <rotational invariance of the Laplacian>.

= 15
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Maclaurin series> $\sinh t=t+t^3/3!+t^5/5!+O(t^7)$, applied with $t=x^2$ and then multiplied by $x$, gives
$$
\boxed{x\sinh(x^2)=x^3+\frac{x^7}{6}+\frac{x^{11}}{120}+O(x^{15}).}
$$
These are the first three nonzero terms. The remainder is written in <Big O notation>.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $u=\ln(1+x)=x-x^2/2+x^3/3+O(x^4)$. Since $u=O(x)$, the outer <Taylor series> requires $u^2=x^2-x^3+O(x^4)$ and $u^3=x^3+O(x^4)$. Therefore
$$
\begin{aligned}
\ln(1+\ln(1+x))&=u-\frac{u^2}{2}+\frac{u^3}{3}+O(u^4)\\
&=x-\frac{x^2}{2}+\frac{x^3}{3}-\frac12(x^2-x^3)+\frac{x^3}{3}+O(x^4)\\
&=\boxed{x-x^2+\frac{7x^3}{6}+O(x^4).}
\end{aligned}
$$
Both <logarithms> are defined on a neighbourhood of zero, so this composition of <power series> is valid there.

= c
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Factor the leading term out of the <Maclaurin series> for the <sine>:
$$
\sin x=x\left(1-\frac{x^2}{6}+\frac{x^4}{120}+O(x^6)\right).
$$
The <binomial theorem> gives, through degree four inside the bracket,
$$
\left(1-\frac{x^2}{6}+\frac{x^4}{120}+O(x^6)\right)^6=1+6\left(-\frac{x^2}{6}+\frac{x^4}{120}\right)+\binom62\frac{x^4}{36}+O(x^6).
$$
The coefficient of $x^4$ is $1/20+5/12=7/15$. Multiplying back by $x^6$ yields
$$
\boxed{\sin^6x=x^6-x^8+\frac7{15}x^{10}+O(x^{12}).}
$$

= 16
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Because $X$ takes values only in $[0,1]$, its <cumulative distribution function> must satisfy $F(1)=1$. Thus
$$
1=A\left(\frac13-\frac14\right)=\frac A{12},\qquad\boxed{A=12}.
$$

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The full <cumulative distribution function> is
$$
\boxed{F(x)=\begin{cases}0,&x<0,\\4x^3-3x^4,&0\leq x\leq1,\\1,&x>1.\end{cases}}
$$
Thus the <function graph> is horizontal at zero for $-1\leq x\leq0$, rises continuously from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1)$, and is horizontal at one for $1\leq x\leq2$. Its <derivative> $12x^2(1-x)$ is positive on $(0,1)$ and zero at both endpoints. The second <derivative> $12x(2-3x)$ changes sign at $x=2/3$, giving an inflection <point> there.

\Image[paper-1-q16-distribution.png]
{title=Cumulative distribution and density, with the mode marked, for Question 16}

= c
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <probability density function> is the <derivative> of the <cumulative distribution function>:
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\begin{cases}12x^2(1-x),&0\leq x\leq1,\\0,&x<0\text{ or }x>1.\end{cases}}
$$
It is nonnegative and has <integral> $F(1)-F(0)=1$. This is a <beta distribution> with parameters $3$ and $2$.

= d
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <probability density function> is zero outside $[0,1]$. Inside that <interval>, it rises from $(0,0)$, reaches its only interior maximum at $x=2/3$, and falls to $(1,0)$. The <derivative> is
$$
f'(x)=12x(2-3x),
$$
positive for $0<x<2/3$ and negative for $2/3<x<1$. Its maximum height is $f(2/3)=16/9$. The second panel of the figure in part (b) shows the <function graph> on the requested full range, with this <mode> marked.

= e
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The interior <stationary point> of the <probability density function> solves $12x(2-3x)=0$ with $0<x<1$, giving $x=2/3$. The sign change of $f'$ established in part (d), together with zero endpoint values, makes this the global maximum. Thus
$$
\boxed{\text{mode}=\frac23,\qquad f(2/3)=\frac{16}{9}.}
$$

= f
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

At the <mode>, the <cumulative distribution function> has value
$$
F(2/3)=4\left(\frac23\right)^3-3\left(\frac23\right)^4=\frac{16}{27}>\frac12.
$$
Since $F$ is strictly increasing on $(0,1)$, its unique crossing of $1/2$ occurs earlier. Therefore **the <median> is less than the <mode>**.

= g
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=g}

The <expected value> is
$$
\mathbb E[X]=\int_0^1x\,12x^2(1-x)\,dx=12\left(\frac14-\frac15\right)=\frac35.
$$
The second moment is $\mathbb E[X^2]=12(1/5-1/6)=2/5$. Hence the <variance> and <standard deviation> are
$$
\operatorname{Var}(X)=\frac25-\left(\frac35\right)^2=\frac1{25},\qquad\boxed{\mathbb E[X]=\frac35,\quad\sigma=\frac15.}
$$

= h
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=h}

The requested bounds are $\mathbb E[X]-\sigma=2/5$ and $\mathbb E[X]+\sigma=4/5$, both within the support. Since the <probability distribution> is continuous, endpoints have zero <probability>, and subtraction of the <cumulative distribution function> gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P\left(\mathbb E[X]-\sigma\leq X\leq\mathbb E[X]+\sigma\right)=4\left[\left(\frac45\right)^3-\left(\frac25\right)^3\right]-3\left[\left(\frac45\right)^4-\left(\frac25\right)^4\right].}
$$
This leaves the answer in rational fractions as requested.

= 17
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

With the standard unweighted <inner product> on real <square-integrable functions>, orthogonality on $[-L,L]$ means
$$
\boxed{\langle f,g\rangle=\int_{-L}^Lf(x)g(x)\,dx=0.}
$$
Multiplying this <inner product> by a common nonzero normalising constant does not change <orthogonality>.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Fourier coefficients> follow by multiplying the expansion by $1$, by the appropriate <cosine>, or by the appropriate <sine>, then using <orthogonality>. In the convention with constant term $a_0/2$,
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
a_0&=\frac1L\int_{-L}^Lh(x)\,dx,\\
a_n&=\frac1L\int_{-L}^Lh(x)\cos\frac{n\pi x}{L}\,dx,\qquad n\geq1,\\
b_n&=\frac1L\int_{-L}^Lh(x)\sin\frac{n\pi x}{L}\,dx,\qquad n\geq1.
\end{aligned}}
$$
The factor $1/L$ comes from the squared <norms> of the nonconstant <sine> and <cosine> functions: the <integral> of each square over $[-L,L]$ is $L$. The constant <function> has squared <norm> $2L$.

= c
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $L=1$, the <even function> $h(x)=x^2$ has $b_n=0$, because $x^2\sin(n\pi x)$ is an <odd function>. Its constant coefficient is $a_0=\int_{-1}^1x^2dx=2/3$. The remaining <Fourier coefficients> are
$$
a_n=2\int_0^1x^2\cos(n\pi x)\,dx.
$$
Two applications of <integration by parts> give the <antiderivative>, with $k=n\pi$,
$$
\frac{x^2\sin(kx)}k+\frac{2x\cos(kx)}{k^2}-\frac{2\sin(kx)}{k^3}.
$$
Its endpoint difference is $2(-1)^n/(n\pi)^2$, so $a_n=4(-1)^n/(n^2\pi^2)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{x^2=\frac13+\frac4{\pi^2}\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^n}{n^2}\cos(n\pi x),\qquad-1\leq x\leq1.}
$$
The periodic extension is continuous and piecewise smooth, so <pointwise convergence of a piecewise smooth Fourier series> gives equality, including at the endpoints.

= d
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

<Termwise differentiation of a Fourier series>, applied to the continuous periodic extension in part (c), gives for $-1<x<1$
$$
2x=-\frac4\pi\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^n}{n}\sin(n\pi x).
$$
Hence the required <Fourier sine series> is
$$
\boxed{x=\frac2\pi\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}n\sin(n\pi x),\qquad-1<x<1.}
$$
The endpoint deserves care: at $x=1$ every term is zero, so the <Fourier series> sums to $0$, the average of the periodic one-sided limits $1$ and $-1$. It therefore does not reproduce the specified endpoint value $g(1)=1$. Changing a value at one <point> does not change any <Fourier coefficient>, so this is still the <Fourier series> of the stated <function> on $(-1,1]$.

= e
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Evaluate the <Fourier sine series> of part (d) at the interior <point> $x=1/2$. Only odd indices remain, with alternating signs:
$$
\frac12=\frac2\pi\left(1-\frac13+\frac15-\cdots\right),\qquad \pi=4\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^k}{2k+1}.
$$
Thus $\boxed{A=4}$.

For the second identity, apply <Parseval's identity> to $h(x)=x^2$. From part (c), $a_0=2/3$, $a_n=4(-1)^n/(n^2\pi^2)$ and $b_n=0$, so
$$
\int_{-1}^1x^4\,dx=\frac12\left(\frac23\right)^2+\frac{16}{\pi^4}\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac1{n^4}.
$$
The left side is $2/5$, giving $\frac{16}{\pi^4}\sum n^{-4}=2/5-2/9=8/45$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\pi^4=90\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac1{n^4},\qquad B=90.}
$$
The first <series> is conditionally convergent, while the second converges absolutely by the <P-series> criterion.

= 18
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <matrix multiplication> $A\mathbf c$ adds each row. Equating it to $\mathbf b$ gives the <linear system>
$$
\alpha+\beta+\gamma=2,\qquad\alpha+3\beta+\gamma=4,\qquad2\alpha+4\beta+2\gamma=6.
$$
The last equation is the sum of the first two, so it supplies no further restriction. Subtracting the first from the second gives $2\beta=2$, hence $\beta=1$, and then $\gamma=1-\alpha$. Thus all parameter choices are
$$
\boxed{\alpha\in\mathbb R,\qquad\beta=1,\qquad\gamma=1-\alpha.}
$$

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Substituting $\beta=1$ and $\gamma=1-\alpha$ into the <matrix> gives
$$
\boxed{A_\alpha=\begin{pmatrix}\alpha+1&1-\alpha&2\\2&\alpha&2-\alpha\\2-\alpha&\alpha+3&1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Its row sums are $4,4,6$, checking that $A_\alpha\mathbf c=\mathbf b$ for every real $\alpha$.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Expand the <determinant> along the first row:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\det A_\alpha={}&(\alpha+1)\left[\alpha-(2-\alpha)(\alpha+3)\right]\\
&-(1-\alpha)\left[2-(2-\alpha)^2\right]+2\left[2(\alpha+3)-\alpha(2-\alpha)\right]\\
={}&(\alpha+1)(\alpha^2+2\alpha-6)-(1-\alpha)(-\alpha^2+4\alpha-2)+2(\alpha^2+6)\\
={}&\boxed{10\alpha^2-10\alpha+8.}
\end{aligned}
$$
The cubic terms cancel in the expansion.

= d
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

<Completing the square> in the <determinant> gives
$$
\det A_\alpha=10\left(\alpha-\frac12\right)^2+\frac{11}{2}>0
$$
for every real $\alpha$. A <square matrix> is invertible exactly when its <determinant> is nonzero, so **$A_\alpha$ is nonsingular for every real $\alpha$**.

= e
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Let $p(t)=\det(tI-A_\alpha)$ be the real <characteristic polynomial>. It is a monic cubic, so $p(t)\to+\infty$ as $t\to+\infty$. At zero,
$$
p(0)=\det(-A_\alpha)=-\det A_\alpha<0.
$$
The <intermediate value theorem> therefore gives some $t_0>0$ with $p(t_0)=0$. A root of the <characteristic polynomial> is an <eigenvalue>, hence **$A_\alpha$ has a positive real <eigenvalue> for every real $\alpha$**. This uses the general fact that an <odd-dimensional real matrix with positive determinant has a positive eigenvalue>.

= 19
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a <series> $\sum u_k$ with eventually nonzero terms, suppose
$$
L=\lim_{k\to\infty}\left|\frac{u_{k+1}}{u_k}\right|
$$
exists, allowing $L=+\infty$. The <ratio test> says that **if $L<1$, the <series> converges absolutely; if $L>1$, it diverges; if $L=1$, the test is inconclusive**. In the divergent case the terms fail to tend to zero. For example, the <P-series> with exponents $1$ and $2$ both have ratio limit $1$ but have different convergence behaviour.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The term at $k=0$ is finite. For every $k\geq1$,
$$
0<\frac1{1+k^2}\leq\frac1{k^2}.
$$
The comparison <P-series> $\sum k^{-2}$ converges, so the <comparison test for series> proves that **the given <series> converges absolutely**.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $u_k=a^{2k+1}/(2k+1)>0$, the consecutive ratio is
$$
\frac{u_{k+1}}{u_k}=a^2\frac{2k+1}{2k+3}\longrightarrow a^2.
$$
Thus the <ratio test> proves convergence when $0<a<1$ and divergence when $a>1$. At $a=1$, the <series> is $\sum_{k\geq0}1/(2k+1)$, which diverges by comparison with $\tfrac12\sum_{k\geq0}1/(k+1)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\text{convergent for }0<a<1;\qquad\text{divergent for }a\geq1.}
$$
All terms are nonnegative, so convergence here is also <absolute convergence>.

= c
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

<L'Hôpital's rule> applies to a quotient $f(x)/g(x)$ of <differentiable functions> near a limiting <point>, excluding that <point> itself, provided $g'(x)\ne0$ there. If the quotient has an indeterminate form $0/0$ or $\infty/\infty$, and the <limit> of $f'(x)/g'(x)$ exists as a finite or infinite value $L$, then
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to a}\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=\lim_{x\to a}\frac{f'(x)}{g'(x)}=L.}
$$
The rule also applies to one-sided <limits> and to limits at infinity, under the corresponding hypotheses. Existence of the derivative-ratio <limit> and the indeterminate-form hypotheses are required; an arbitrary quotient cannot be replaced by a quotient of <derivatives>.

= d
{parent=19}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write the product as a quotient with indeterminate form $(-\infty)/(+\infty)$. By <L'Hôpital's rule>,
$$
\lim_{x\to0^+}x\ln x=\lim_{x\to0^+}\frac{\ln x}{1/x}=\lim_{x\to0^+}\frac{1/x}{-1/x^2}=\lim_{x\to0^+}(-x)=\boxed{0}.
$$

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The original PDF specifies $x\to a$. The quotient is the <difference quotient> for the <function> $f(x)=x^x=e^{x\ln x}$ at the positive <point> $a$. The <chain rule> gives $f'(x)=x^x(\ln x+1)$, so
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to a}\frac{x^x-a^a}{x-a}=a^a(1+\ln a),\qquad a>0.}
$$

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use the <root limit of a finite sum of powers>. Put $M=\max\{1,a,a^2/2\}\geq1$. For every $x>0$, at least one of the three terms is $M^x$ and none is larger, so
$$
M^x\leq1+a^x+(a^2/2)^x\leq3M^x.
$$
Taking the positive $x$th root gives
$$
M\leq\left(1+a^x+(a^2/2)^x\right)^{1/x}\leq3^{1/x}M.
$$
Since $3^{1/x}\to1$, the <squeeze theorem> gives
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to\infty}\left(1+a^x+(a^2/2)^x\right)^{1/x}=\begin{cases}1,&0\leq a\leq1,\\a,&1<a\leq2,\\a^2/2,&a>2.\end{cases}}
$$
Indeed, for $a\leq1$ both remaining bases are at most $1$; for $1\leq a\leq2$, $a\geq a^2/2$; and for $a\geq2$, $a^2/2\geq a$. The endpoint cases $a=0,1,2$ are included in the same bound.

= 20
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a differentiable objective $f$ and regular constraint $g=r$, a constrained <stationary point> satisfies
$$
\boxed{\nabla f=\lambda\nabla g,\qquad g=r,}
$$
where $\lambda$ is a <Lagrange multiplier>. Solve these equations for both the coordinates and $\lambda$.

Geometrically, the <gradient> $\nabla g$ is normal to the constraint <level set> when $\nabla g\ne0$. Every feasible infinitesimal displacement $\mathbf v$ is tangent to that <level set>, so $\nabla g\cdot\mathbf v=0$. At a constrained <stationary point>, the directional change $\nabla f\cdot\mathbf v$ must also vanish for every such tangent displacement. Thus $\nabla f$ is normal to the same tangent <plane> and is a <scalar multiple> of $\nabla g$. This is the geometric basis of the <Lagrange multiplier> method.

For a second constraint $h=s$, with <linearly independent vectors> $\nabla g$ and $\nabla h$, the feasible tangent directions are perpendicular to both. Their normal space is the <linear span> of these two <gradients>, so introduce a second <Lagrange multiplier>:
$$
\boxed{\nabla f=\lambda\nabla g+\mu\nabla h,\qquad g=r,\quad h=s.}
$$
These equations find candidates; deciding whether they are maxima, minima or <saddle points> requires further analysis. Constraint <points> where the required gradient independence fails must be examined separately.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <angles> in the PDF are measured from the vertical normal to the interface. The horizontal displacements of the two path segments are $d_1\tan\theta$ and $d_2\tan\phi$, while their <lengths> are $d_1\sec\theta$ and $d_2\sec\phi$. Thus the geometric constraint and travel <time> are
$$
g(\theta,\phi)=d_1\tan\theta+d_2\tan\phi=D,\qquad T(\theta,\phi)=\frac{2d_1\sec\theta+\sqrt2d_2\sec\phi}{c}.
$$
The <Lagrange multiplier> equations $\nabla T=\lambda\nabla g$ give
$$
\frac{2d_1}{c}\sec\theta\tan\theta=\lambda d_1\sec^2\theta,\qquad\frac{\sqrt2d_2}{c}\sec\phi\tan\phi=\lambda d_2\sec^2\phi.
$$
Cancelling the positive geometric factors gives $\lambda=2\sin\theta/c=\sqrt2\sin\phi/c$, hence
$$
\boxed{\sin\phi=\sqrt2\sin\theta.}
$$
This is <Snell's law>, $\sin\theta/(c/2)=\sin\phi/(c/\sqrt2)$. The relation depends only on the ratio of the two <speeds>, so changing $D,d_1,d_2$ leaves the relation unchanged; it changes which <angles> satisfy it together with the geometric constraint.

For a minimum check, let $u$ be the horizontal distance to the crossing <point>. Then
$$
T(u)=\frac2c\sqrt{u^2+d_1^2}+\frac{\sqrt2}{c}\sqrt{(D-u)^2+d_2^2},
$$
and
$$
T''(u)=\frac{2d_1^2}{c(u^2+d_1^2)^{3/2}}+\frac{\sqrt2d_2^2}{c((D-u)^2+d_2^2)^{3/2}}>0.
$$
Thus the travel <time> is a <strictly convex function> of $u$, and its <stationary point> is the unique global minimum. For positive $D,d_1,d_2$, $T'(0)<0<T'(D)$, placing the crossing between the two endpoint projections.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Put $s=\sin\theta$. By part (i), $\sin\phi=\sqrt2s$. The acute-angle geometry requires $0<s<1/\sqrt2$. Expressing the <tangents> in terms of their <sines> in the geometric constraint gives the required <implicit equation>
$$
\boxed{\frac{\sqrt3s}{\sqrt{1-s^2}}+\frac{\sqrt2s}{\sqrt{1-2s^2}}=2.}
$$
The hint suggests $s=1/m$ for an integer $m\geq2$. Testing $m=2$ gives
$$
\frac{\sqrt3/2}{\sqrt3/2}+\frac{1/\sqrt2}{1/\sqrt2}=1+1=2.
$$
Thus $\boxed{\sin\theta=1/2}$. Each term on the left is strictly increasing in $s$ over the allowed <interval>, so this solution is unique.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The acute <angles> follow from part (ii) and the <Snell's law> relation:
$$
\boxed{\theta=\pi/6=30^\circ,\qquad\phi=\pi/4=45^\circ.}
$$
The path <lengths> below and above the interface are respectively
$$
\ell_1=\frac{\sqrt3}{\cos(\pi/6)}=2,\qquad\ell_2=\frac1{\cos(\pi/4)}=\sqrt2.
$$
Hence the total distance and minimum travel <time> are
$$
\boxed{\ell=2+\sqrt2,\qquad T_{\min}=\frac2{c/2}+\frac{\sqrt2}{c/\sqrt2}=\frac6c.}
$$
For a sketch to scale, choose the interface as $y=0$, with $A=(0,-\sqrt3)$ and $B=(2,1)$. The crossing is $C=(1,0)$ because $d_1\tan\theta=d_2\tan\phi=1$. The figure uses equal axis scales and shows the two normal-relative <angles>.

